A1. These belong to the older set but trot around with the younger set. ~ GRANDSONS
A9. A pointed argument. ~ DUEL
A10. If you buy these you will make a sound investment. ~ RADIOS
A12. This is a relief. (plur.) ~ CAMEOS
A14. This will bring "Judge" for ten weeks. ~ DOLLAR
A16. An old style filling station. ~ BAR
A17. This is something to a door. ~ KNOB
A19. This one is always holding the bag. ~ REDCAP
A21. Inquires. ~ ASKS
A23. The garden of yesterday. ~ EDEN
A25. An eagle (crossword species.) ~ ERNE
A26. What we all need on blue Monday. ~ CHEER
A28. This is what Mr. Edison did with his birthday cake. ~ ATE
A29. This is hard to get on a holiday. ~ REST
A30. If this word were used to describe a Scotchman we would say he was close. ~ NEAR
A32. Deeply engrossed. ~ RAPT
A35. A period. ~ DOT
A36. This printer's measure always comes in handy. ~ EM
A38. These are always trying to get the company started. ~ PROMOTERS
A41. This comes after do-re. ~ MI
A42. This is what they are trying to put on in Chicago. ~ LID
A44. Let the editor do this. ~ EDIT
A45. This is pretty soft (for a mineral). ~ TALC
A47. These are single. ~ ONES
A49. A bit of femininity. ~ SHE
A51. You can always find the key in a flat here. ~ PIANO
A53. You will often see this on a nut. ~ RUST
A54. Ocean travelers. ~ TARS
A56. An entreaty. ~ PLEA
A57. The way a Yank sometimes gets a base. ~ STEALS
A60. These should never be allowed to get in a head. ~ LICE
A62. This is sometimes allowed. ~ LET
A63. Roving. ~ ERRANT
A65. This should be obliterated. ~ ERASED
A67. Wearisomeness. ~ TEDIUM
A69. This is what the boss did with the best looking typewriter. ~ USED
A70. This is all dated up for next fall. ~ SEPTEMBER

D1. The foundation of the Wrigley building in Chicago. ~ GUM
D2. This is what those well known onions do. ~ REEK
D3. The way the newlyweds like to live. ~ ALONE
D4. Poor health keeps this man going. (abbr.) ~ DR
D5. The way a clown often feels under his paint. ~ SAD
D6. You will find this down where the violets grow. ~ ODOR
D7. A river in Africa. ~ NILE
D8. This helps to keep the lid on. ~ SOLDER
D9. What smoke will do to Chicago. ~ DARKEN
D11. The way the marriage vow used to be. ~ SACRED
D12. A woman's buy-word. ~ CASH
D13. This looks good in glasses. ~ SODA
D15. The price the enemy has to pay. ~ RANSOM
D16. These do not let a wife dictate. ~ BACHELORS
D18. This is what kept Col. Lindbergh from making a non-stop fight over the sea of matrimony. ~ BETROTHAL
D20. Grandmother used to wear this long—long ago. ~ PETTICOAT
D22. Percolate. ~ SEEP
D24. Spruce. ~ NEAT
D27. This is never well done. ~ RARE
D31. This is very slender and straight. ~ ROD
D33. What "sweethearts on parade" should never do. ~ PET
D34. This is a bad place to get caught. ~ TRAP
D37. It will take quite a few seconds to make this. ~ MINUTE
D39. This is hard to see through. ~ MIST
D40. This could be made from a banana peel. ~ SLIP
D43. This has lots of grit. ~ DESERT
D46. This is what they did to his bluff. ~ CALLED
D48. What the crowd does when a 1929 model passes. ~ STARES
D50. This reaches from Buffalo to Toledo. ~ ERIE
D52. Want. ~ NEED
D55. Many women would get down on their knees in order to do this. ~ SCRUB
D58. Dip. ~ LADE
D59. What a pair of clippers will do. ~ SNIP
D61. The wealthy often ride in this. ~ EASE
D64. An old dried up king. ~ TUT
D66. A light weight in India. ~ SER
D68. A personal pronoun. ~ ME


